/*Logic:

1. Initialize sum = 1 because every child must receive at least one candy.

2. Start traversing the ratings array from index 1.

3. If the current rating is equal to the previous rating:

- Give the current child 1 candy.
- Add 1 to the total sum.
- Move to the next child.

4. If the current rating is greater than the previous rating:

- An increasing slope is formed.
- Initialize peak = 1.
- Keep increasing peak by 1 and add it to sum until the increasing slope ends.

5. If the current rating is smaller than the previous rating:

- A decreasing slope is formed.
- Initialize down = 1.
- Keep increasing down by 1 and add it to sum until the decreasing slope ends.

6. After processing both slopes, compare peak and down:

- If down is greater than peak, add (down - peak) to sum.
- This adjustment ensures that the peak child gets more candies than both neighboring children.

7. Continue until all children are processed.

8. Return sum as the minimum total number of candies required.

Time Complexity: O(N), because we traverse the array only once.

Space Complexity: O(1), because we use only a few variables and no extra array.
*/

class Solution {
public:
    int candy(vector<int>& ratings) {
        int n = ratings.size();

        // Every child gets at least one candy
        int i = 1, sum = 1;

        while (i < n) {

            // Case 1: Equal ratings
            // Both children can have the same number of candies
            if (ratings[i] == ratings[i - 1]) {
                sum += 1;
                i++;
                continue;
            }

            // Case 2: Increasing slope
            // Keep increasing candies as ratings increase
            int peak = 1;

            while (i < n && ratings[i] > ratings[i - 1]) {
                peak++;
                sum += peak;
                i++;
            }

            // Case 3: Decreasing slope
            // Keep increasing the required candies
            // while moving down the slope
            int down = 1;

            while (i < n && ratings[i] < ratings[i - 1]) {
                sum += down;
                down++;
                i++;
            }

            // If the decreasing slope is longer than the
            // increasing slope, the peak needs extra candies
            // to satisfy both neighboring children.
            if (down > peak) {
                sum += down - peak;
            }
        }

        return sum;
    }
};
